From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pkgdemon <jpm820@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 01:56:58 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 82/82] NextBSD: extent-map swap pager: swapon(2) on a local
 UFS regular file

sys_swapon() accepts a regular file only on a network filesystem, so a
local swap file has to go through md(4), whose kthread is not pageproc
and sleeps in vm_wait() inside VOP_WRITE behind the pagedaemon it is
supposed to serve.

Accept a regular file on UFS and swap on it with the filesystem off the
I/O path, as Linux (swap_activate) and xnu (kern_open_file_for_direct_io)
do:

- At swapon the file is pinned: VOP_FSYNC(MNT_WAIT), so no soft-updates
  dependency is pending, then vinvalbuf(), which drops its cached
  buffers and pages.  VOP_BMAP is walked once into a table of physical
  extents on the provider below the mount; a hole refuses swapon
  (EINVAL, "preallocate it").  VIRF_SWAP is then set, so the file can
  no longer be written, truncated, unlinked or renamed.
- swapfile_strategy() translates each swap block through the table and
  sends bios to the filesystem's own g_vfs consumer, as
  swapgeom_strategy() does for a partition: writes allocate with
  g_new_bio() and never sleep, and a buf that crosses an extent
  boundary is split under a counting parent bio.
- g_vfs_io_start()/g_vfs_io_done(), split out of g_vfs_done(), count
  that I/O in the g_vfs softc so an orphaned provider is torn down only
  after it completes; after an orphan the swap I/O fails with ENXIO.
- swap_pager_mount_busy(): dounmount() refuses even a forced unmount,
  and vfs_domount_update() a downgrade to read-only, while a file on
  the mount is in use for swap.  swapon holds vfs_busy() while it pins,
  so a racing unmount either waits and then sees the swap device or
  makes swapon fail.  Shutdown already runs swapoff_all() before
  vfs_unmountall().
- swapoff waits for in-flight I/O, clears VIRF_SWAP, drops the file's
  cache again, and closes and releases the vnode.

The extent map is rebuilt at every swapon and never cached.  Bypass
writes skip soft updates and UFS snapshot copy-on-write, which both
hook buffer-cache writes in ffs_geom_strategy(); a snapshot's view of
the swap file therefore holds live swap contents, which nothing that
reads a snapshot interprets.

Only UFS is accepted.  ZFS and other local filesystems still fail with
ENOTBLK exactly as before, and NFS keeps its swapdev path.  No
structure layout or existing function signature changes: the swap
file's state hangs off swdevt's opaque sw_id, and the new interfaces
are additions.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 sys/geom/geom_vfs.c  |  40 ++-
 sys/geom/geom_vfs.h  |   3 +
 sys/kern/vfs_mount.c |  22 +-
 sys/vm/swap_pager.c  | 588 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 sys/vm/swap_pager.h  |   2 +
 5 files changed, 650 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sys/geom/geom_vfs.c b/sys/geom/geom_vfs.c
index 122e2f6a0..0e0f13e16 100644
--- a/sys/geom/geom_vfs.c
+++ b/sys/geom/geom_vfs.c
@@ -97,10 +97,8 @@ static void
 g_vfs_done(struct bio *bip)
 {
 	struct g_consumer *cp;
-	struct g_event *event;
 	struct g_vfs_softc *sc;
 	struct buf *bp;
-	int destroy;
 	struct mount *mp;
 	struct vnode *vp;
 	struct cdev *cdevp;
@@ -168,6 +166,42 @@ g_vfs_done(struct bio *bip)
 	bp->b_resid = bp->b_bcount - bip->bio_completed;
 	g_destroy_bio(bip);
 
+	g_vfs_io_done(cp);
+
+	bufdone(bp);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Account for I/O that a kernel client (the NextBSD swap-file pager)
+ * sends straight to a filesystem's consumer, bypassing the buffer
+ * cache, so that an orphaned provider is torn down only after it
+ * completes.  g_vfs_io_start() fails with ENXIO once the provider is
+ * gone; each successful call is paired with one g_vfs_io_done().
+ */
+int
+g_vfs_io_start(struct g_consumer *cp)
+{
+	struct g_vfs_softc *sc;
+
+	sc = cp->geom->softc;
+	mtx_lock(&sc->sc_mtx);
+	if (sc->sc_orphaned || sc->sc_enxio_active) {
+		mtx_unlock(&sc->sc_mtx);
+		return (ENXIO);
+	}
+	sc->sc_active++;
+	mtx_unlock(&sc->sc_mtx);
+	return (0);
+}
+
+void
+g_vfs_io_done(struct g_consumer *cp)
+{
+	struct g_event *event;
+	struct g_vfs_softc *sc;
+	int destroy;
+
+	sc = cp->geom->softc;
 	mtx_lock(&sc->sc_mtx);
 	destroy = ((--sc->sc_active) == 0 && sc->sc_orphaned);
 	if (destroy) {
@@ -178,8 +212,6 @@ g_vfs_done(struct bio *bip)
 	mtx_unlock(&sc->sc_mtx);
 	if (destroy)
 		g_post_event_ep(g_vfs_destroy, cp, event, NULL);
-
-	bufdone(bp);
 }
 
 void
